<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 6 (Due October 8)</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete all the exercises below and submit your work by the due date.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
           Suppose <m>X</m> is a finite set. Explain why a sequence of functions that converges pointwise on <m>X</m> also converges uniformly on <m>X</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
           Give an example to show that Egorov's Theorem can fail without the hypothesis that <m>\mu(X) &lt; \infty</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
        Suppose that <m>F_1, F_2, \ldots F_n</m> are disjoint closed subsets of <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. Prove that if
        <md>
            g: F_1 \cup F_2 \cup F_n \to \mathbf{R}
        </md>
        is a function such that <m>g|_{F_k}</m> is a continuous function for all <m>k \in \{1,2, \ldots\}</m> then <m>g</m> is a continuous function.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>g: \mathbf{R} \to \mathbf{R}</m> be defined by
          <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  \sin(\frac{1}{x}) \amp \text{ if $x \neq 0$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x =0$} 
  \end{cases}
          </md>
          Let <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>. Find a closed set <m>F</m> such that <m>|\mathbf{R}\setminus F| &lt; \epsilon</m> and <m>g|_F</m> is a continuous function on <m>F</m>.
        </p>

      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give an example of a Borel measurable function <m>f: \mathbf{R}\to \mathbf{R}</m> such that there does not exist a closed set <m>F \subseteq \mathbf{R}</m> such that <m>|\mathbf{R \setminus }F| = 0</m> and <m>f|_F</m> is a continuous function on <m>F</m>.
          </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>